= Biological cell
{wiki=Cell_(biology)}

A <biological cell> is a membrane-bounded basic unit of living organization. In a cell-level experiment it can be an <experimental unit>, provided that treatment assignment and relevant interactions are handled at that level.

The shared architecture of cellular life combines a selective <cell membrane>, internal <cytoplasm>, inherited information in <DNA>, and <ribosomes> that assemble <proteins>. <Metabolism> supplies transformations of matter and <energy> needed for maintenance and growth. These components form a coupled system: a boundary retains useful <molecules>, <enzymes> organize <chemical reactions>, and inherited information supports rebuilding the machinery. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: prokaryotic cells].

A worked structural comparison: in a typical <prokaryote>, <DNA> occupies a region without a surrounding <cell nucleus>; in a typical nucleated <eukaryote>, much of the <DNA> lies inside a membrane-bounded <cell nucleus>. Both still have a <cell membrane>, <cytoplasm> and <ribosomes>. The extra boundary compartmentalizes processes; it does not create a different basic principle of living organization. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: eukaryotic cells].

\Image[cell-compartments.png]
{title=Shared cellular architecture with different DNA compartmentalization}
{description=Original simplified schematics of a <prokaryote> and a nucleated <eukaryote>. Colors identify corresponding components; sizes and component counts are illustrative, and additional structures are omitted.}

Shared architecture describes cellular life and its developmental machinery, rather than requiring every specialized <biological cell> to retain every component. A mature mammalian <red blood cell>, for example, lacks a <cell nucleus>, although it develops from nucleated precursors. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/40-2-components-of-the-blood[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: components of blood]. This distinction makes cellular unity compatible with striking specialization.

= Cell line
{parent=Biological cell}

= HeLa
{parent=Cell line}
{c}
{wiki}

= Cell state
{parent=Biological cell}

A <cell state> describes current properties of a <biological cell>, including its <gene expression>, structure and activity. A snapshot of <cell state> does not alone establish its future <cell fate>.

= Stem cell
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <stem cell> can produce both renewed <stem cells> and differentiated descendants. Its developmental range depends on the type and setting; self-renewal does not imply unrestricted <cell fate>.

= Progenitor cell
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <progenitor cell> is a precursor with relatively restricted differentiation potential and, generally, more limited self-renewal than a <stem cell>. Usage depends on the lineage and assay; an expression marker alone does not establish renewal capacity.

= Secretory granule
{parent=Biological cell}

A storage compartment that participates in regulated <exocytosis>; the <insulin> example distinguishes storage of a <hormone> from storage of <glycogen> fuel.

= Cell cycle
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

The <cell cycle> is the sequence of events by which a <biological cell> prepares for and completes division, including <DNA replication> and distribution of <chromosomes>. In <Saccharomyces cerevisiae>, changing bud appearance provides an accessible readout; see the Burgess, Powers and Mell account and Hartwell, Culotti and Reid's primary study cited there.

For a typical cycling <diploid> <eukaryotic> <biological cell>, the order is <G1 phase>, <S phase>, <G2 phase>, then <M phase>. <DNA replication> doubles nuclear <DNA> amount during <S phase>; <mitosis> distributes the copies, and <cytokinesis> separates the daughters. The useful distinction is between copying a library and allocating its copies: <DNA replication> changes how much material exists, whereas division changes which <biological cell> contains it.

\Image[cell-cycle-phases-and-dna-content.png]
{title=The <cell cycle>: copying nuclear <DNA> once, then distributing it to two daughters}
{description=Original schematic for a typical cycling <diploid> <eukaryotic> <biological cell>. Each box states nuclear <DNA> content and <chromosome> count; <M phase> is summarized by its completed daughters. Arrow lengths do not represent phase durations. The <G0 phase> branch shows reversible withdrawal where re-entry is possible, rather than claiming that all nondividing <biological cells> can return.}

<Gene expression> continues to supply <RNA> and <proteins> as the <biological cell> grows; it is different from duplicating the <genome>. In the <yeast cross-species complementation experiment>, restored growth requires successful repeated <cell cycles>, but growth alone does not locate a defect within a phase. Bud appearance in <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> provides another readout, while a <flow cytometric DNA-content assay> asks how much nuclear <DNA> is present and <DNA pulse labeling> asks whether new <DNA> was synthesized during an exposure.

= Interphase
{parent=Cell cycle}
{wiki}

<Interphase> comprises <G1 phase>, <S phase> and <G2 phase>, including growth and <DNA replication>.

= G1 phase
{c}
{parent=Interphase}
{wiki}
{title2=$G_1$}

During <G1 phase>, growth and preparation precede <DNA replication>. The ordinary <diploid> nuclear state is $2n,2C$.

= S phase
{c}
{parent=Interphase}
{wiki}
{title2=$S$}

During <S phase>, nuclear <DNA replication> produces paired <sister chromatids>. <DNA> content rises from $2C$ to $4C$, while the <chromosome> count remains $2n$.

= G2 phase
{c}
{parent=Interphase}
{wiki}
{title2=$G_2$}

<G2 phase> follows completed nuclear <DNA replication> and precedes <mitosis>. The ordinary <diploid> state is $2n,4C$. <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/The Cell Cycle>[Biology 2e, §10.2, Interphase and its G1, S and G2 subsections; Figure 10.5]{ref}

= M phase
{c}
{parent=Cell cycle}
{title2=$M$}

<M phase> includes <mitosis> and ordinarily <cytokinesis>, which can overlap in time.

= Mitosis
{parent=M phase}
{wiki}

<Mitosis> allocates copied <chromosomes> to daughter <cell nuclei>. The <spindle apparatus> aligns <sister chromatids> before separation in <anaphase>.

The copying has already occurred during <S phase>, before <mitosis>. Nuclear <chromosome segregation> and <cytokinesis>, the partition of the <cytoplasm>, are distinct processes: two daughter <cell nuclei> can form inside one undivided <biological cell>.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/The Cell Cycle>[§10.2, Interphase and The Mitotic Phase; Figure 10.5: replication precedes nuclear division, which can occur without cytokinesis]{ref}

The original cartoon follows a hypothetical <diploid> <animal> <biological cell> with $2n=4$. Its two <homologous chromosome> pairs are $A,a$ and $B,b$; upper- and lower-case letters identify homologues, not dominant and recessive <alleles>. In <prophase> and <metaphase>, each replicated <chromosome> contains two <sister chromatids>: four replicated <chromosomes> contain eight <chromatids>, rather than eight replicated <chromosomes>. The X shape depicts two associated copies, not the shape of every <chromosome> throughout the <cell cycle>.

\Image[mitosis-animal-chromosome-partition.png]
{title=<Mitosis> partitions replicated <chromosomes>; <cytokinesis> separates the daughters}
{description=Original schematic of a hypothetical $2n=4$ <animal> <biological cell>, drawn with open <mitosis>. Colors and letters follow the four original <chromosomes>; paired strokes represent <sister chromatids>. Every <anaphase> pole and each completed daughter receives $A,a,B,b$. Green lines represent selected <spindle apparatus> <microtubules>; dark dots mark <centromere> regions. The intermediate <prometaphase> is described below. Geometry and arrow spacing are illustrative, not recorded images or elapsed times.}

This open <mitosis> cartoon shows <nuclear envelope> breakdown and reassembly. Closed <mitosis> in some <yeasts> retains the <nuclear envelope> while daughter <chromosomes> separate inside it; the cartoon is therefore not a universal cellular architecture.<The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper>[Chapter 8, The Nucleus during Mitosis, Dissolution of the Nuclear Envelope; Figures 8.29–8.30: open and closed mitosis]{ref}

= Prophase
{parent=Mitosis}
{wiki}

In <prophase>, replicated <chromosomes> condense and the <spindle apparatus> begins to organize.

= Prometaphase
{parent=Mitosis}
{wiki}

In open <mitosis>, <nuclear envelope> breakdown permits <spindle apparatus> <microtubules> to reach <kinetochores> at <centromere> regions.

= Metaphase
{parent=Mitosis}
{wiki}

In <metaphase>, replicated <chromosomes> align near the <spindle apparatus> equator, with their two <sister chromatids> connected through <kinetochores> toward opposite poles.

= Anaphase
{parent=Mitosis}
{wiki}

In mitotic <anaphase>, <sister chromatids> separate into daughter <chromosomes> moving toward opposite poles.

Release of <cohesin>-mediated association permits the sister <centromere> regions to separate. Each former <chromatid> is then counted as an independent daughter <chromosome>. This changes the counting convention without making additional <DNA> copies. For the $2n=4$ example, the whole undivided <biological cell> transiently contains eight daughter <chromosomes>, four moving toward each pole. Movement toward poles and separation of the poles both contribute to partition in the illustrated <animal> setting.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/The Cell Cycle>[§10.2, Karyokinesis, Figure 10.6 and Anaphase: release of cohesion, daughter chromosomes and spindle elongation]{ref}

= Telophase
{parent=Mitosis}
{wiki}

In open <mitosis>, <telophase> includes reassembly of <nuclear envelopes> around the separated <chromosome> groups and relaxation of their condensation.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/The Cell Cycle>[§10.2, Telophase and Figure 10.6]{ref}

= Mitotic daughter chromosome accounting
{parent=Mitosis}
{title2=$2n=4$}

Count the same hypothetical <diploid> <animal> <biological cell> in three ways: <chromosomes>, double-stranded <DNA> <molecules>, and nuclear <DNA> amount. Assume one continuous double-stranded <DNA> <molecule> per unreplicated <chromosome> or replicated <chromatid>, complete <S phase>, and no losses or <mutations>. Exclude <mitochondrion> <DNA>. Here $n=2$ is the size of one <haploid> set, and $C$ is its unreplicated nuclear <DNA> amount, as defined in <cell-cycle DNA content>. A double-stranded <DNA> <molecule> counts as one, despite containing two strands.

\Table
[
\Tr[\Th[Stage and compartment]\Th[<Chromosomes>]\Th[Double-stranded <DNA> <molecules>]\Th[Nuclear <DNA> amount]]
\Tr[\Td[One <G1 phase> parent]\Td[4 unreplicated]\Td[4]\Td[$2C$]]
\Tr[\Td[One <prophase> or <metaphase> <biological cell>]\Td[4 replicated]\Td[8 in 4 pairs of <sister chromatids>]\Td[$4C$]]
\Tr[\Td[Whole undivided <anaphase> <biological cell>]\Td[8 separated daughter <chromosomes>]\Td[8]\Td[$4C$ total]]
\Tr[\Td[One <anaphase> pole's allocation]\Td[4 daughter <chromosomes>]\Td[4]\Td[$2C$ allocated]]
\Tr[\Td[Each daughter after <mitosis> and <cytokinesis>]\Td[4 unreplicated]\Td[4]\Td[$2C$ each]]
]

\Image[mitosis-daughter-chromosome-dna-accounting.png]
{title=Four replicated <chromosomes>, eight <DNA> <molecules>, then four of each per daughter}
{description=Original accounting diagram for $2n=4$, counting nuclear <DNA> only. Each colored stroke represents one double-stranded <DNA> <molecule>; the small bridge in a replicated pair indicates association, not a new <DNA> <molecule>. The eight separate strokes in the whole <anaphase> <biological cell> are already partitioning into two groups. The final row states counts per daughter, not their sum.}

The allocation can be checked copy by copy. <DNA replication> turns $A$ into $A_1,A_2$, $a$ into $a_1,a_2$, and similarly for $B,b$. One daughter receives $A_1,a_1,B_1,b_1$ and the other $A_2,a_2,B_2,b_2$. Each therefore inherits the same four <chromosome> identities and the same <gene> and <allele> content as the unreplicated parent, under the stated assumptions. Across both daughters there are $4+4=8$ <DNA> <molecules> and $2C+2C=4C$: partition conserves the material present after <S phase>.

The transient whole-cell count of eight does not mean that each daughter's <ploidy> has doubled. Each completed daughter is <diploid>, containing both homologues of each <homologous chromosome> pair. This differs from separating <homologous chromosomes> during <meiosis I>. Faithful <mitosis> preserves a nuclear <genome> through growth and renewal; equal inherited <genes> need not imply equal <gene expression> or equal <cell fate>. <Mutations> or <chromosome segregation> errors can break the ideal inheritance pattern.

= Spindle checkpoint
{parent=Mitosis}
{wiki}

The <spindle checkpoint> can delay <anaphase> while <kinetochore> attachment is incomplete. This couples progression to the state of the <spindle apparatus>, rather than requiring every <biological cell> to separate its <sister chromatids> after a fixed waiting time. Alignment in a picture alone is not a demonstration that this control is satisfied.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/Control of the Cell Cycle>[Biology 2e, §10.3, Regulation at Internal Checkpoints, Figure 10.11 and The M Checkpoint]{ref}

= Kinetochore ablation experiment
{parent=Spindle checkpoint}

In 1995, <Conly L. Rieder>, <Richard W. Cole>, <Alexey Khodjakov> and <Greenfield Sluder> tested whether an unattached <kinetochore> supplies the inhibitory signal delaying <anaphase>. Living PtK1 <biological cells> on glass coverslips were mounted in Rose chambers at $35$–$37\,{}^\circ\mathrm{C}$. An inverted microscope with a $60\times$ objective recorded <differential interference contrast microscopy> video. A pulsed $532\,\mathrm{nm}$ <laser> focused through that objective destroyed selected <chromosome> regions. The readout was <chromosome> movement and <anaphase> onset before and after surgery.<The checkpoint delaying anaphase in response to chromosome monoorientation is mediated by an inhibitory signal produced by unattached kinetochores by Conly L. Rieder and others>[pp. 941–942, Abstract and Materials and Methods: Cell Culture; Laser Microsurgery and Video-enhanced Light Microscopy]{ref}

Destroying the last unattached <kinetochore> permitted <anaphase> without that <chromosome> aligning. Cutting <chromosome> arms served as a damage control: progression still awaited attachment of the last naturally one-pole-attached <chromosome>. Correlative <electron microscopy> checked the absence of the targeted <kinetochore>. Together these results located an inhibitory activity in or near the unattached <kinetochore>, rather than establishing a requirement for alignment itself.<The checkpoint delaying anaphase in response to chromosome monoorientation is mediated by an inhibitory signal produced by unattached kinetochores by Conly L. Rieder and others>[pp. 943–947, arm-cutting control, Table I, Figures 1, 5 and 6; destruction of the last unattached kinetochore and correlative electron microscopy]{ref}

This intervention localized control; it did not identify every signaling <molecule> or guarantee faithful inheritance after damage. The cultured PtK1 preparation differs from the hypothetical $2n=4$ cartoon. Neither the cartoon nor this experiment establishes identical control in every <species>.

= Cytokinesis
{parent=M phase}
{wiki}

<Cytokinesis> partitions the <cytoplasm>. Without <cytokinesis>, two <cell nuclei> can share one <biological cell>. <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/The Cell Cycle>[Biology 2e, §10.2, The Mitotic Phase, Figure 10.6 and Cytokinesis]{ref}

= G0 phase
{c}
{parent=Cell cycle}
{wiki}
{title2=$G_0$}

<G0 phase> describes withdrawal from the division cycle. Some <biological cells> re-enter <G1 phase>; others remain nondividing. <Metabolism> and <gene expression> can continue. <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/The Cell Cycle>[Biology 2e, §10.2, G0 Phase]{ref} In the ordinary <diploid> case, $2C$ alone cannot distinguish <G0 phase> from <G1 phase>.

= Cell-cycle DNA content
{parent=Cell cycle}
{title2=$(2n,2C)\to(2n,4C)\to2\,(2n,2C)$}

Let $n$ count <chromosomes> in one complete set, and let $C$ denote the nuclear <DNA> amount in one unreplicated <haploid> set of this <organism>. <Ploidy> counts sets, whereas $C$ units count material; $C$ is not a universal mass shared by all <species>. Here we count a paired replicated <chromosome> as one until its <sister chromatids> separate. A copied <diploid> <genome> is therefore still <diploid> through <S phase>: $2n$ persists even as $2C$ becomes $4C$.

An original bookkeeping example uses a hypothetical <diploid> <biological cell> with $n=3$. Name its three <homologous chromosome> pairs $A,a$, $B,b$ and $D,d$. Assume ordinary nuclear <DNA replication>, no <chromosome> loss, and one continuous double-stranded <DNA> <molecule> per unreplicated <chromosome> or replicated <chromatid>. Count nuclear material only, excluding <mitochondrion> <DNA>.

\Table
[
\Tr[\Th[Stage and counting compartment]\Th[<Chromosomes>]\Th[Double-stranded <DNA> <molecules>]\Th[Nuclear <DNA> amount]]
\Tr[\Td[One <G1 phase> <biological cell>]\Td[6 unreplicated]\Td[6]\Td[$2C$]]
\Tr[\Td[One <G2 phase> <biological cell>]\Td[6 replicated]\Td[12 in 6 pairs of <sister chromatids>]\Td[$4C$]]
\Tr[\Td[Whole undivided <anaphase> <biological cell>]\Td[12 separated daughter <chromosomes>]\Td[12]\Td[$4C$ total]]
\Tr[\Td[Each completed daughter <cell nucleus>]\Td[6 unreplicated]\Td[6]\Td[$2C$ each]]
]

For example, $A$ becomes the paired copies $A_1,A_2$ during <S phase>. They remain one replicated <chromosome> until separation. Each completed daughter receives one $A$ copy and one $a$ copy, and similarly for the other two pairs. Across both daughters, $2\times6=12$ double-stranded <DNA> <molecules> and $2(2C)=4C$: division allocates the copied material without another round of <DNA replication>.

The whole undivided <anaphase> <biological cell> transiently contains $4n$ separately counted daughter <chromosomes>. This counting change does not establish a four-set <ploidy> in a completed daughter <genome>: each pole receives $2n$, and each completed daughter <cell nucleus> is <diploid>. Always state whether a number refers to one <cell nucleus>, one pole, or the entire undivided <biological cell>. Compare <meiotic DNA content>: after <meiosis I>, a <haploid> <cell nucleus> can contain $2C$, so amount alone cannot determine <ploidy>.

= Cell cycle analysis
{parent=Cell cycle}
{wiki}

<Cell cycle analysis> assigns evidence to stages of the <cell cycle>. A <flow cytometric DNA-content assay> measures accumulated nuclear <DNA>, whereas <DNA pulse labeling> detects synthesis over an exposure window. Agreement between these readouts is useful because an amount and a synthesis event answer different questions.

= Double thymidine block
{parent=Cell cycle analysis}

= DNA pulse labeling
{c}
{parent=Cell cycle analysis}

<DNA pulse labeling> exposes <biological cells> to a detectable precursor for a bounded interval. After removal, later sampling can follow the labeled material and descendants. A short pulse asks about synthesis during that interval; overnight labeling instead accumulates evidence across many hours. Detectable synthesis does not by itself prove completion of a <cell cycle>, and <DNA repair> can also incorporate precursors.

= DNA synthesis labeling with EdU
{c}
{parent=DNA pulse labeling}

= EdU labeling
{c}
{synonym}

<EdU labeling> uses a modified <nucleoside>, 5-ethynyl-2'-deoxyuridine, incorporated into newly synthesized <DNA>, then chemically couples it to a fluorescent reporter.

A 2008 experiment injected adult mice with $100\,\mathrm{\mu g}$ of this label and inspected intestinal sections 96 hours later. Fixed sections on slides received $10\,\mathrm{\mu M}$ Alexa568-azide for 10 minutes. Imaging with a <fluorescence microscope> combined the reporter image with a Hoechst <DNA> counterstain and a transmitted-light view. Labeled <cell nuclei> marked descendants of <biological cells> synthesizing <DNA> during exposure, rather than identifying current <S phase> at collection. <A chemical method for fast and sensitive detection of DNA synthesis in vivo by Adrian Salic and Timothy J. Mitchison>[A chemical method for fast and sensitive detection of DNA synthesis in vivo, Figure 4 legend: mouse pulse, section preparation and imaging]{ref}

The same study's cultured-cell controls compared overnight $10\,\mathrm{\mu M}$ label, no label, and label with $10\,\mathrm{mM}$ <hydroxyurea>. No-label samples had little reporter background; <hydroxyurea> strongly suppressed incorporation. The camera detected residual signal with a 3-second exposure versus 40 milliseconds under otherwise identical settings, demonstrating why acquisition settings matter. These controls support synthesis-dependent labeling, without proving that every labeled <biological cell> completes division. <A chemical method for fast and sensitive detection of DNA synthesis in vivo by Adrian Salic and Timothy J. Mitchison>[Figure 2 legend, A–M: NIH 3T3 controls, fluorescence and differential interference contrast imaging, exposure-time comparison]{ref}

= Spindle apparatus
{parent=Cell cycle}

The <spindle apparatus> distributes <chromosomes> during division of a <cell nucleus>.

= Cell envelope
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

The <cell envelope> comprises a <biological cell>'s surrounding boundary layers, including its <cell membrane> and a <cell wall> or additional outer structures when present. The term is especially useful when discussing <bacteria>, where the layers must be distinguished.

= Bacterial outer membrane
{parent=Cell envelope}
{wiki}

A <bacterial outer membrane> is an additional membrane outside the <peptidoglycan> in typical <Gram-negative bacteria>. It adds a barrier while the inner <cell membrane> remains the boundary of the <cytoplasm>.

= Cell wall
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <cell wall> is a supporting structure outside a <cell membrane>. It helps preserve shape and bears the load when <osmosis> raises internal <pressure>. The <cell membrane> supplies the <selective permeability> described in its own article; the <cell wall> supplies mechanical reinforcement. These functions cooperate: a porous supporting layer can restrain expansion without being the boundary that retains dissolved <solutes>. <Turgor pressure> makes this cooperation visible in a firm leaf; <plasmolysis> and a <lysozyme wall-removal experiment> separate the two functions experimentally.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/5.2 Passive Transport>[Biology 2e, §5.2, Tonicity in Living Systems, Figure 5.13: wall support and plasmolysis]{ref}

The material is not universal. Most <bacteria> use <peptidoglycan>, whose linked <sugar> chains and <amino acid> cross-links are explained below. A <plant cell wall> uses <cellulose>; a <fungal cell wall> contains <chitin> and <glucans>. <Animals> lack a <cell wall>, some <bacteria> are naturally wall-free, and <archaea> have different wall chemistry. Thus a <cell wall> is neither a universal cellular component nor one chemical recipe.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/22.2 Structure of Prokaryotes: Bacteria and Archaea>[Biology 2e, §22.2, Figure 22.13 and The Cell Wall of Prokaryotes: wall-free bacteria and archaeal differences]{ref}<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/4.3 Eukaryotic Cells>[Biology 2e, §4.3, Animal Cells versus Plant Cells and The Cell Wall: cellulose and animal comparison]{ref}<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/24.1 Characteristics of Fungi>[Biology 2e, §24.1, Cell Structure and Function: chitin and glucans]{ref}

\Image[cell-envelope-comparison.png]
{title=Representative <cell envelopes>: different supporting layers outside the <cell membrane>}
{description=Original layer-order schematic comparing <Gram-positive bacteria>, <Gram-negative bacteria>, a <plant cell wall>, a <fungal cell wall>, a member of <archaea> with a protein wall and an <animal> cell. Colors distinguish a <cell wall> from a <cell membrane> and a <bacterial outer membrane>. Thicknesses and spacing are illustrative, not measurements. These examples do not exhaust variation within any group.}

The <bacterial outer membrane> in typical <Gram-negative bacteria> is an additional membrane outside the <peptidoglycan>, not another name for that supporting mesh. The <cell envelope> includes the boundary layers together. An extra barrier can change access to the <peptidoglycan>, so response to <lysozyme> cannot be inferred from the mere presence of a <cell wall>.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/22.2 Structure of Prokaryotes: Bacteria and Archaea>[Biology 2e, §22.2, Figure 22.16: inner membrane, peptidoglycan and outer membrane]{ref}

= Plant cell wall
{parent=Cell wall}

A <plant cell wall> provides reinforcement outside the <cell membrane>, with <cellulose> as a major structural component. It is a composite rather than a solid sheet of pure <cellulose>. Wall construction varies with the kind and developmental state of the <biological cell>.

= Fungal cell wall
{parent=Cell wall}

A <fungal cell wall> contains structural <polysaccharides> including <chitin> and <glucans>. Its composition and organization vary among <fungi>; the diagram illustrates a composite rather than a pure <chitin> shell. A <fungus> sharing a <cell wall> with a <plant> does not imply shared wall chemistry.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/24.1 Characteristics of Fungi>[Biology 2e, §24.1, Cell Structure and Function: structural wall polysaccharides]{ref}

= Chitin
{parent=Fungal cell wall}
{tag=Polysaccharide}
{wiki}

<Chitin> is a structural <polysaccharide> built from modified <sugar> units. It contributes reinforcement to a <fungal cell wall>.

= Glucan
{parent=Fungal cell wall}
{tag=Polysaccharide}
{wiki}

A <glucan> is a <polysaccharide> built from <glucose> units. Different linkages produce different structures; some <glucans> contribute to a <fungal cell wall>.

= Turgor pressure
{parent=Cell wall}
{wiki}
{title2=$P_{\mathrm{in}}-P_{\mathrm{out}}$}

<Turgor pressure> is the internal <hydrostatic pressure> above the external <pressure> in a <biological cell>. When <water> enters a walled <biological cell> by <osmosis>, expansion loads the <cell wall>. Rising <pressure> can oppose further net entry while the <cell membrane> retains its <selective permeability>. Mechanical support therefore does not require the <cell wall> to block <water>.

As an original mechanical check, idealize a small flat patch of <cell wall> of <surface area> $A$ under uniform <turgor pressure> $\Delta P=P_{\mathrm{in}}-P_{\mathrm{out}}$. Its outward <force> is $F=\Delta P A$. Doubling $A$ at fixed <pressure> doubles the load; equal internal and external <pressures> give zero net pressure load. This is a force balance, not a measured wall strength or a complete model of a curved, growing <biological cell>. It says nothing about which <solutes> cross the <cell membrane>.

= Plasmolysis
{parent=Cell wall}
{wiki}

<Plasmolysis> is withdrawal of the membrane-bounded contents from parts of a <cell wall> after <water> loss. A sufficiently concentrated external <hypertonic solution>, with <solutes> effectively excluded by the <cell membrane> over the observation time, can cause this. The <cell wall> remains; the enclosed material contracts. <Plasmolysis> is therefore distinct from <osmotic lysis>, which breaks the cellular boundary during excessive swelling. A return to a less concentrated surrounding <chemical solution> can reverse <plasmolysis> if the <biological cell> remains functional.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/5.2 Passive Transport>[Biology 2e, §5.2, Tonicity in Living Systems and Figure 5.13: membrane withdrawal under hypertonic conditions]{ref}

\Image[cell-wall-osmotic-experiment-logic.png]
{title=<Plasmolysis> with a retained <cell wall>, and osmotic challenge after wall removal}
{description=Original qualitative experiment logic. The upper pair compares a turgid <plant> cell with <plasmolysis> in an external <hypertonic solution>. The lower pair compares a supported <protoplast> with <osmotic lysis> after dilution for a susceptible preparation. Arrows indicate interventions or possible net <water> movement, not measured rates. The dilution comparison concerns <lysozyme>-susceptible <bacteria>; the upper comparison concerns <plant> cells. No measured dimensions, times or survival fractions are implied.}

= Protoplast
{parent=Cell wall}
{wiki}

A <protoplast> is the living contents of a <biological cell> bounded by its <cell membrane> after removal of the <cell wall>. Removing the <cell wall> need not immediately destroy the <cell membrane>; stability depends on the preparation and surroundings. <Lysozyme> acts on susceptible bacterial <peptidoglycan>, not on <cellulose> or <chitin>, so it is not a universal wall-removal <enzyme>.

= Lysozyme wall-removal experiment
{parent=Protoplast}

The 1953 paper <The isolation of protoplasts from Bacillus megaterium by controlled treatment with lysozyme by Claes Weibull> (pp. 688–690, Figures 2–3) tested <Bacillus megaterium> strain KM. Apparatus included a Klett-Summerson colorimeter with a red filter and a Spencer no. 8 <phase-contrast microscope>. <Lysozyme> in phosphate buffer produced empty residual bodies; adding $0.2\,\mathrm{M}$ <sucrose> preserved granular spheres. Diluting their surroundings caused rapid <osmotic lysis>.

The same study compared untreated, boiled, <lysozyme>-exposed and <sucrose>-protected samples. Filled, stoppered tubes reduced damage from air exposure. After <centrifugation>, a Beckman DU spectrophotometer measured ultraviolet <wave absorption> of the separated liquid. Protected samples resembled untreated controls in releasing little ultraviolet-absorbing material; boiled and unprotected samples released much more. Later-growth cultures reduced spontaneous breakdown as a confounder.<The isolation of protoplasts from Bacillus megaterium by controlled treatment with lysozyme by Claes Weibull>[pp. 688–689, Materials and Methods; pp. 692–694, release comparison and Figure 6]{ref}

Together, the shape and release readouts support removal of mechanical protection while a permeability barrier persists. They do not establish unchanged <selective permeability> to every <solute>, long-term viability, or universal susceptibility.

An instructive extension of this experimental logic is a matched comparison: <lysozyme> present or absent, crossed with osmotic support present or absent. Keep starting material, buffer, observation interval and handling comparable; include a blank to distinguish optical effects of reagents from changes in the <biological cells>. Then dilute already formed <protoplasts> as a separate intervention. This separates access and wall removal from failure after removal. These are proposed controls for interpreting the mechanism, not additional reported results from the historical paper. A fall in optical signal alone cannot establish rupture: shape changes also alter <scattering of light>.

= Peptidoglycan
{parent=Cell wall}
{wiki}

<Peptidoglycan> is a mesh of linked <sugar> chains cross-linked by short chains of <amino acids> in the <cell walls> of most <bacteria>. Its absence from the <cell walls> of <archaea> helps distinguish envelope chemistry without confusing either <cell wall> with a <cell membrane>. See https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-3-unique-characteristics-of-prokaryotic-cells[OpenStax Microbiology, 3.3: cell walls and peptidoglycan].

= Cell membrane
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

= Plasma membrane
{synonym}

The <cell membrane>, also called the <plasma membrane>, is the selective boundary separating a <biological cell>'s <cytoplasm> from its surroundings. Both <prokaryotes> and <eukaryotes> have a <cell membrane>. Where a <cell wall> is present, it is a distinct structure outside the <cell membrane>; the two names are not synonyms. This separation lets a <biological cell> maintain internal conditions while exchanging material through <membrane transport>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Cell-Membrane-Plasma-Membrane[NHGRI: cell membrane].

The familiar architecture is a <lipid bilayer> containing <membrane proteins>. In a <phospholipid>-based <lipid bilayer>, two <membrane leaflets> face opposite ways: <hydrophilic> heads face the <water> on each side, while <hydrophobic> tails face one another in the interior. The heads are therefore exposed both outside and inside the <biological cell>. This arrangement combines a water-facing surface with a barrier to many water-soluble <solutes>; the schematic represents organization rather than an atomistic structure. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26871/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the lipid bilayer].

\Image[cell-membrane-selective-barrier.png]
{title=Two <membrane leaflets> and selective routes through a <cell membrane>}
{description=Original <cell membrane> schematic showing <phospholipids>, <hydrophilic> heads, <hydrophobic> tails, an <ion channel>, and a <carrier protein>. Arrows illustrate possible passage, not measured rates. Shapes, sizes, spacing and numbers of <molecules> are illustrative.}

<Selective permeability> means that different substances cross at different rates under comparable conditions. Small <nonpolar> <molecules> can pass through a <lipid bilayer> by <diffusion>, while <ions> encounter a strong barrier without a suitable route. An <ion channel> supplies a selective water-filled pathway when open; a <carrier protein> binds a <solute> and changes shape to expose it to the other side. These <membrane proteins> therefore add specific routes to the barrier. An open <ion channel> permits <passive transport>, whereas a <carrier protein> can mediate either <passive transport> or <active transport>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26815/[Alberts et al.: principles of membrane transport].

The useful combination is retention plus controlled exchange: a completely impermeable boundary would prevent uptake, whereas unrestricted mixing would erase differences in <concentration> and <membrane potential>. The <fluid mosaic model> connects these functions to an organized but mobile <lipid bilayer> containing <proteins>.

= Selective permeability
{parent=Cell membrane}

<Selective permeability> means that a <cell membrane> permits different substances to pass at different rates under comparable conditions. Both the <lipid bilayer> and the available <membrane proteins> contribute to this selection.

Artificial boundaries can also have <selective permeability>. For <osmosis>, specify which <solutes> cross and over what observation interval: a boundary that retains one <solute> need not retain another. A <semipermeable membrane> is the ideal solvent-only boundary used in the <osmotic pressure> calculation.

= Semipermeable membrane
{parent=Selective permeability}
{wiki}

A <semipermeable membrane> ideally passes the <solvent> while excluding the specified <solutes>. In the aqueous <osmosis> model below, <water> can cross but the counted dissolved particles cannot. Real <selective permeability> is often a matter of different rates, so this idealization needs checking over the measurement interval. Neither an impermeable sheet nor a freely mixing opening realizes this model.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/osmotic pressure>[Gold Book, osmotic pressure: definition of the solvent-only membrane]{ref}

= Membrane protein
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

= Membrane transport protein
{parent=Membrane protein}
{wiki}

= GLUT4
{c}
{parent=Membrane transport protein}
{wiki}

= Carrier protein
{parent=Membrane transport protein}

A membrane <carrier protein> binds a <solute> and changes its exposed binding site from one side of a <cell membrane> to the other. Depending on its mechanism and coupling, it can mediate <passive transport> or <active transport>.

= Gap junction
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

= Ion channel
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

An <ion channel> is a <membrane protein> that provides a selective pathway for <ions> across a <cell membrane>. An open pore permits <passive transport> down the relevant <electrochemical gradient>; it does not supply the <free energy> for uphill transport as a pump involved in <active transport> does. Two things are needed for a net <electric current>: an available pathway and an <electrochemical potential> difference. Opening a pore alone need not produce a net <electric current>. See <Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Ion Channels and the Electrical Properties of Membranes>.

The <selective permeability> of an <ion channel> depends on interactions between its pore and the passing <ions>, not simply on a hole's diameter. Some <ion channels> strongly favor <potassium> over <sodium>; others admit several kinds of positively charged <ions>. Thus selective does not necessarily mean exclusive to one <ion> species. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11123/[Purves et al., Neuroscience, 2nd edition (2001): molecular structure of ion channels] and the mixed-channel example in https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11013/[Purves et al.: principles derived from studies of the neuromuscular junction].

\Image[ion-channel-selective-passage.png]
{title=An open <ion channel> supplies a selective passive pathway}
{description=Original model schematic: selected positive <ions> can traverse a pore in a <membrane protein>, while the differently shaped particles are excluded from this modeled pore. The arrow shows one possible net direction when the corresponding <electrochemical gradient> favors entry; the pore does not enforce that direction. Shapes, particle counts and dimensions are illustrative, not physical microscopic data or a universal selectivity rule.}

Gating is the opening and closing of an <ion channel>'s pathway. A <voltage-gated ion channel> responds to <membrane potential>; an <ionotropic receptor> can respond to a bound <ligand>. Gating changes the available <electrical conductance>, while the <electrochemical gradient> supplies the drive for <passive transport>. For a fixed <reversal potential> in the simple model below, opening more identical <ion channels> increases the magnitude of the <electric current> at a fixed nonzero drive, without choosing its sign independently of <membrane potential>. The <two-state ion-channel gating> model describes how the open fraction can change. See the Alberts reference above.

For a worked approximation, hold an open population's <electrical conductance> at $g=10\,\mathrm{nS}$ and its <reversal potential> at $E_{\mathrm{rev}}=-80\,\mathrm{mV}$. Use $V=V_{\mathrm{inside}}-V_{\mathrm{outside}}$ for <membrane potential> and \b[inward-positive <conventional current>]:
$$
I_{\mathrm{in}}=g(E_{\mathrm{rev}}-V),\qquad g\geq0.
$$
At $V=-100,-80,-60\,\mathrm{mV}$, respectively, the <electric currents> are $+200,0,-200\,\mathrm{pA}$, since $1\,\mathrm{nS}\times1\,\mathrm{mV}=1\,\mathrm{pA}$. Positive <electric current> means net positive <electric charge> entering, or net negative <electric charge> leaving; it does not mean that every <ion> moves inward. For a pore passing only positive <ions>, the three results mean net inward passage, no net passage, and net outward passage. These stipulated values describe an illustrative Ohmic approximation, not measured properties of a particular <ion channel>; see <reversal potential> for the current plot and limitations.

The useful connection is between a molecular gate and a controllable <electric current>. A <ligand> can change <electrical conductance> through an <ionotropic receptor>, or <membrane potential> can change it through a <voltage-gated ion channel>, connecting recognition and electrical signaling. In a historical landmark, Erwin Neher and Bert Sakmann reported currents from individual <ion channels> in frog muscle membranes in 1976. See https://doi.org/10.1038/260799a0[Neher and Sakmann, Single-channel currents recorded from membrane of denervated frog muscle fibres, Nature 260, 799–802 (1976)].

= ATP-sensitive potassium channel
{c}
{parent=Ion channel}
{wiki}

= Membrane transport
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

<Membrane transport> is the passage of substances across a <cell membrane>. For the small <solutes> considered here, distinguish the available route from its driving force: a <lipid bilayer>, <ion channel>, or <carrier protein> determines how passage can occur, while differences in <chemical potential> and <membrane potential> determine which direction is energetically downhill. A large driving force cannot make an unavailable route conduct. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26815/[Alberts et al.: principles of membrane transport].

For an ideal dilute <chemical solution> at uniform <temperature> and <pressure>, with the same reference molar <chemical potential> on both sides, the molar <electrochemical potential> change for transferring a <solute> from compartment A to B is
$$
\Delta\widetilde\mu_{\mathrm{A}\to\mathrm{B}}=RT\ln\!\left(\frac{c_{\mathrm B}}{c_{\mathrm A}}\right)+zF(V_{\mathrm B}-V_{\mathrm A}).
$$
Here $c_{\mathrm A},c_{\mathrm B}$ are <concentrations>, $T$ is absolute <temperature>, $R$ is the <molar gas constant>, $z$ is signed <ion> charge number, $F$ is the <Faraday constant>, and $V_{\mathrm B}-V_{\mathrm A}$ is the <electric potential> difference. The <natural logarithm> contains a dimensionless ratio. The electrical term vanishes for an uncharged <solute>. More generally, use <thermodynamic activities> in place of the ideal <concentration> ratio. This combines the ideal-solution <chemical potential> difference with electrical <energy> per mole; see https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/E01945[IUPAC Gold Book: electrochemical potential]. For a <biological cell>, taking B inside and A outside makes the voltage term use the existing <membrane potential> convention.

\Image[membrane-transport-gradients.png]
{title=<Passive transport> and <active transport> for a stipulated <concentration> difference}
{description=Ten illustrative neutral <solute> tracers are split 8:2 between equal fixed-<volume> compartments. The lower sketches compare conceptual <free energy> profiles for <passive transport> and the <solute> part of <active transport>. Heights are unscaled and are not experimental barriers; the lower-right curve excludes the driving process, so it is not a full coupled-cycle <free energy> profile.}

= Passive transport
{parent=Membrane transport}
{wiki}

<Passive transport> is net movement down the transported substance's <electrochemical gradient> without coupling that movement to another source of <free energy>. <Facilitated diffusion> is <passive transport> through an <ion channel> or a passive <carrier protein>. Providing such a route can speed passage, but does not turn an uphill transfer into a downhill one. For an uncharged <solute> under the ideal conditions above, net <passive transport> runs from higher to lower <concentration>; for an <ion>, the <membrane potential> also matters. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9847/[Cooper, The Cell: transport of small molecules].

In the figure, stipulate eight neutral <solute> tracers in A and two in B, with equal fixed <volumes>, identical surroundings, and no imposed <electric potential> difference. The <concentration> ratio is $c_{\mathrm A}/c_{\mathrm B}=8/2=4$, so A-to-B transfer has $\Delta\widetilde\mu=-RT\ln4\simeq-1.38629RT$, or $-3.458\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ at the assumed <temperature> $T=300\,\mathrm K$. If this <solute> can cross, <thermodynamic equilibrium> has equal expected <concentrations>: five tracers per side on average. Individual crossings occur in both directions; an observed ten-particle snapshot need not contain exactly five on each side. These stipulated counts illustrate a driving force, not a measured flow rate or a prediction of the time to equilibrate.

For a separate illustrative <ion> calculation, keep $c_{\mathrm A}/c_{\mathrm B}=4$, but assign $z=+1$ and $V_{\mathrm A}-V_{\mathrm B}=-60\,\mathrm{mV}$ at $300\,\mathrm K$. Using $R\simeq8.31446\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ and $F\simeq96485.33\,\mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$, B-to-A transfer gives $RT\ln4-0.060F\simeq3.458-5.789=-2.331\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Thus an appropriate open <ion channel> can permit <passive transport> toward the higher <concentration>: the electrical contribution outweighs the opposing <chemical potential> contribution. This stipulated <membrane potential> is not inferred from the tracer counts.

= Facilitated diffusion
{parent=Passive transport}
{wiki}

<Facilitated diffusion> is <passive transport> through a <membrane transport protein>, such as an <ion channel> or <carrier protein>, down the relevant <electrochemical gradient>. The <protein> supplies a route; it does not by itself provide <free energy> for uphill transfer.

= Osmosis
{parent=Passive transport}
{wiki}

<Osmosis> is net movement of <water> across a boundary whose permeability differs for <water> and relevant <solutes>. The direction depends on the <chemical potential> of <water>, including both dissolved material and <pressure>. A <cell wall> can let <water> reach the <cell membrane>, yet oppose expansion through <turgor pressure>. Zero net movement does not mean individual <water> <molecules> have stopped crossing.

Imagine pure <water> on side A and a <chemical solution> on side B of a <semipermeable membrane>. At equal <temperature> and <pressure>, dissolved particles lower the <chemical potential> of <water> in B, and net <water> transfer initially favors A to B. Retaining the <solutes> lets <water> redistribute without simply mixing everything. Increasing B's <pressure> opposes entry; enough <pressure> gives <thermodynamic equilibrium> with unequal <solute> <concentrations>. This is why “<water> moves toward more <solute>” requires a pressure condition.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/5.2 Passive Transport>[§5.2, Osmosis and Mechanism: selective passage and pressure opposing entry]{ref}

\Image[osmosis-membrane-pressure-balance.png]
{title=<Selective permeability> and the <pressure> that balances <osmosis>}
{description=Original schematic of a boundary passing <water> and retaining the illustrated <solute>. Left: equal <pressures> allow net transfer toward the <chemical solution>. Right: excess <pressure> on the solution side balances the osmotic drive while <water> exchanges in both directions. Equal opposing arrows denote zero net transfer, not a measured molecular rate. Particle counts and dimensions are illustrative.}

At <thermodynamic equilibrium>, the <chemical potentials> of exchangeable <water> are equal. Individual <water> <molecules> still move and cross in both directions, with equal mean opposing transfer rates and zero net <flux>. A stationary liquid level alone does not prove this: a blocked route can also give no visible transfer. <Selective permeability> provides a route; <water potential> supplies the direction, and neither alone determines the speed. The <cell wall>, <turgor pressure> and <plasmolysis> articles show why a living boundary's mechanical support and its <selective permeability> must be distinguished.

= Water potential
{parent=Osmosis}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Psi$}

<Water potential> expresses the <chemical potential> of <water> relative to a stated pure-<water> reference in units of <pressure>. For a nearly incompressible liquid at fixed <temperature>, use a constant reference <molar volume> $\bar V_w$ and write
$$
\Psi=\frac{\mu_w-\mu_w^{\mathrm{ref}}}{\bar V_w}.
$$
The reference is pure <water> at the same <temperature> and a chosen <pressure> $P_0$. Both sides must use the same reference. Neglecting height differences and interactions with solid surfaces, the approximation is
$$
\mu_w\simeq\mu_w^{\mathrm{ref}}+RT\ln a_w+\bar V_w(P-P_0),\qquad
\Psi\simeq(P-P_0)+\frac{RT}{\bar V_w}\ln a_w=\Psi_p+\Psi_s.
$$
Here $a_w$ is the dimensionless <thermodynamic activity> of <water>, $R$ the <molar gas constant>, and $T$ the absolute <temperature>. The <natural logarithm> is therefore applied to a dimensionless quantity. The <pressure> contribution $\Psi_p=P-P_0$ increases with <pressure>; the <solute potential> $\Psi_s$ is negative when $a_w<1$. Across an available passive water route, net <water> transfer favors higher to lower <water potential>. The familiar <plant> description adds effects of height and interactions with solid surfaces when they matter; they are omitted in the two liquid compartments here.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/30.5 Transport of Water and Solutes in Plants>[Biology 2e, §30.5, Water Potential, Solute Potential, Pressure Potential and Figure 30.32: component balance and direction of transfer]{ref}

Adding <solute> and raising <pressure> can thus cancel in <water potential> while each contribution remains nonzero. Equality of <water potential> does not imply equality of <pressure> or of every <solute> <concentration>. In a supported <plant> cell, the <cell wall> permits a positive <turgor pressure> to balance a negative <solute potential>; <plasmolysis> illustrates the opposite loss of support after <water> leaves.

= Solute potential
{parent=Water potential}
{title2=$\Psi_s$}

= Osmotic potential
{synonym}

<Solute potential> is the contribution to <water potential> from dissolved material, with pure <water> assigned zero. In the nearly incompressible description, $\Psi_s=(RT/\bar V_w)\ln a_w$. In a dilute ideal <chemical solution>, $\Psi_s\simeq-RTc_{\mathrm{osm}}$, where $c_{\mathrm{osm}}$ is the <osmotic concentration> of dissolved particles. Thus <solute potential> is negative while the corresponding <osmotic pressure> is positive: $\Psi_s\simeq-\Pi$. This sign difference distinguishes a contribution to <water potential> from the compensating mechanical <pressure>.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/30.5 Transport of Water and Solutes in Plants>[Biology 2e, §30.5, Solute Potential: negative concentration contribution]{ref}

= Osmotic pressure
{parent=Osmosis}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Pi$}

<Osmotic pressure> is the excess <pressure> needed on a <chemical solution> to maintain osmotic <thermodynamic equilibrium> with pure <solvent> across a <semipermeable membrane>. For a nearly incompressible <solvent>, equality of <chemical potential> gives $\Pi\simeq-(RT/\bar V_w)\ln a_w$. It is an equilibrium property; an observed initial swelling rate is a different measurement.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/osmotic pressure>[Gold Book, osmotic pressure: equilibrium definition, solvent activity formula and dilute ideal limit]{ref}

For a dilute ideal <chemical solution> of retained particles,
$$
\Pi=\frac{nRT}{V}=RTc_{\mathrm{osm}}.
$$
Here $n$ is the <amount of substance> of independently dissolved <solute> particles, measured in moles; $V$ is the final <volume> of <chemical solution>, not the starting <volume> of pure <solvent>. Count the resulting <ions> separately if a <solute> dissociates; do not multiply twice if $n$ already counts them. With $c_{\mathrm{osm}}$ in $\mathrm{mol\,m^{-3}}$ and $R$ in $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, $RTc_{\mathrm{osm}}$ has units $\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}}=\mathrm{Pa}$. This resembles the <ideal gas law>, although the particles here are dissolved in a liquid.

The assumptions are uniform <temperature>, a dilute ideal <chemical solution>, a nearly incompressible <solvent>, and a <semipermeable membrane> that retains the counted <solutes> while passing <solvent>. Association, incomplete dissociation and interactions change the relation between added material and independent particles; nonideal <thermodynamic activities> invalidate simple particle counting. The formula does not describe a concentrated brine merely because the apparatus contains a membrane.

A short derivation explains why particles are counted. In an ideal mixture, $a_w=x_w=n_w/(n_w+n)$, with $n_w$ the <amount of substance> of <water>. If $n\ll n_w$, then $-\ln x_w=\ln(1+n/n_w)\simeq n/n_w$ and $V\simeq n_w\bar V_w$. Substitution gives $\Pi\simeq RTn/V$. This is a dilute-limit approximation, not a claim that <water> <molecules> stop moving or that dissolved particles are a gas.

= Osmosis concentration-pressure example
{parent=Osmotic pressure}

Take two reservoirs separated by a <semipermeable membrane> that passes <water> and retains one neutral <solute>. Stipulate $T=300\,\mathrm K$, $c_A=10\,\mathrm{mol\,m^{-3}}$ and $c_B=30\,\mathrm{mol\,m^{-3}}$, equivalent to $10$ and $30\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}}$. Treat both <chemical solutions> as dilute and ideal. These are invented model conditions, not measurements or recommended biological conditions.

For <pressures> measured at the same height beside the membrane, define $\Delta P=P_B-P_A$. The model predicts
$$
\Psi_A-\Psi_B=RT(c_B-c_A)-\Delta P,\qquad
RT(c_B-c_A)\simeq49.89\,\mathrm{kPa}.
$$
At $\Delta P=0$, net <water> transfer favors A to B. At $\Delta P\simeq49.89\,\mathrm{kPa}$, the opposing <pressure> balances the <osmotic pressure> difference, giving zero net transfer with unequal <concentrations>. At $\Delta P=75\,\mathrm{kPa}$, the sign reverses and net <water> transfer favors B to A, toward the more dilute reservoir. Raising <pressure> changes the total <water potential>, rather than changing the membrane into a one-way gate.

\Image[osmosis-concentration-pressure-cases.png]
{title=Stipulated <concentration> and <pressure> conditions determine net <water> transfer}
{description=Original computed comparison for ideal retained <solute> at $300\,\mathrm K$, with A at $10\,\mathrm{mol\,m^{-3}}$ and B at $30\,\mathrm{mol\,m^{-3}}$. The horizontal axis is $P_B-P_A$; the vertical axis is $\Psi_A-\Psi_B$. Three marked cases show forward transfer, balance and reversed transfer. These are instantaneous reservoir conditions, not a time course or measured biological data; slope does not specify permeability or speed.}

Fixed <concentrations> require large maintained reservoirs or an instantaneous comparison. In a closed <membrane osmometer>, entering <water> causes <dilution> of the receiving <chemical solution> and changes its <volume>; the final balance must use final <concentrations>. A supported <cell wall> provides another way to develop the opposing <pressure>, as explained under <turgor pressure>.

= Membrane osmometer
{parent=Osmosis}
{wiki}

A <membrane osmometer> connects <osmosis> to a visible liquid level or a measured balancing <pressure>. In a teaching version, a <solute>-containing pouch is joined to a narrow vertical tube and submerged in external <water>. Entry increases the enclosed <volume> and raises the meniscus. Both the column and any stretched pouch can contribute an opposing <pressure>.

The University of Toronto's <Osmosis (Brine) by University of Toronto Department of Physics> demonstration (activity Me-012b, Description, steps 1–14) specifies soaking a dialysis membrane tube, tying one end closed and fastening the other around a thistle tube's narrow end. A stand holds the tube. Colored brine is cooled to room <temperature> and injected using a syringe to reduce bubbles; the pouch is completely immersed in a $1$ or $2\,\mathrm L$ beaker of distilled <water>. A taped marker records the initial level; subsequent meniscus height is the readout. The protocol proposes another pouch containing distilled <water> as a comparison, and its older instructions explicitly check seals for leaks.<Osmosis (Brine) by University of Toronto Department of Physics>[Osmosis (Brine), Description steps 1–14, water-filled comparison and Old Description A.2: apparatus, readout and leak check]{ref}

A rise is consistent with net <water> entry. To infer <osmotic pressure> quantitatively, use a retained <solute> and establish a stable equilibrium after accounting for <dilution>. If both free surfaces are at the same atmospheric <pressure>, the liquids have approximately equal <mass densities> $\rho$, and the pouch adds negligible mechanical load, the <hydrostatic pressure> difference at membrane height is approximately $\Delta P=\rho gh$, where $h$ is the meniscus height above the external surface and $g$ is <gravitational acceleration>. This is the liquid-column inference, not a pressure calibration supplied by the Toronto demonstration. For unequal <mass densities>, compare the two column pressures separately.

Useful additional controls are matched initial levels and geometry, constant <temperature>, replicate runs, and monitoring external <solute> to test retention. These are proposed checks, not reported Toronto results. Leaks, trapped bubbles, <surface tension> in the narrow tube, <evaporation> and membrane stretching can alter the level. In particular, a dialysis membrane may pass dissolved salt: the brine setup illustrates transient movement but cannot be assumed to retain every dissolved <ion>. Color improves visibility without proving salt retention. A stationary level can reflect weakening <concentration> differences or leakage rather than osmotic <thermodynamic equilibrium>; a rising level alone supplies neither $n$ nor a verified value of $nRT/V$.

= Osmotic concentration
{parent=Osmosis}
{wiki}
{title2=$c_{\mathrm{osm}}$}

= Osmolarity
{synonym}

In the dilute ideal particle-count description, <osmolarity> is the total <amount of substance> of independently dissolved <solute> particles per <volume> of <chemical solution>: $c_{\mathrm{osm}}=\sum_j n_j/V$. It includes both membrane-permeating and retained <solutes>; specifying a particular membrane is unnecessary to count particles. A neutral undissociated <solute> contributes one particle per <molecule>, whereas separated <ions> are counted individually. Common units are $\mathrm{osmol\,L^{-1}}$ or $\mathrm{mOsm\,L^{-1}}$, with one osmole denoting one mole of the counted particles. <Osmolality> uses mass of <solvent> instead of <volume> of <chemical solution>; the two numerical values should not be identified without the appropriate mass-to-volume conversion.<Osmosis, Cell Membranes-Structure and Transport, BYU-Idaho>[Cell Membranes—Structure and Transport, Osmosis: paragraphs beginning Earlier this semester and Osmolarity is a useful term; total particle counting]{ref}

This ideal counting definition states its scope: interactions can require a <thermodynamic activity> description of <osmotic pressure>. <Tonicity> asks a different question, namely how that surrounding <chemical solution> changes a specified <biological cell>'s <volume> through its particular <cell membrane>.

= Tonicity
{parent=Osmosis}
{wiki}

<Tonicity> describes how a surrounding <chemical solution> changes cellular <volume> through <osmosis>. It depends on which <solutes> remain effectively excluded on the timescale of interest, not simply on the amount of dissolved material. The following comparisons take a functional <cell membrane>, an initially unpressurized <biological cell>, and effectively excluded <solutes> as the reference; rising <turgor pressure> can oppose entry in a walled <biological cell>.

Operationally, <hypotonic solutions> cause sustained swelling, <hypertonic solutions> sustained shrinking, and <isotonic solutions> no sustained <volume> change in the specified passive comparison. State the reference <biological cell>, membrane properties and observation interval. A <permeating solute> can transiently change <water potential> while crossing; after its <chemical potential> equilibrates, its initial unequal <concentrations> cannot be treated as a permanently retained osmotic load. Thus equal <osmolarities> need not imply <isotonic solutions>. Conversely, a stationary walled <biological cell> may have unequal <osmolarities> balanced by <turgor pressure>.<Osmosis, Cell Membranes-Structure and Transport, BYU-Idaho>[Cell Membranes—Structure and Transport, Osmosis: paragraphs beginning Here is another way and final Note; permeating solutes and transient effects]{ref}

= Permeating solute
{parent=Tonicity}

A <permeating solute> can cross the specified membrane appreciably during the observation interval. This is a property of a <solute>-membrane-time comparison, not an immutable label attached to a <molecule>. Passage can reduce a <concentration> difference through <passive transport> while <water> is also moving; it does not require <water> to wait.

For an original dilute ideal example, stipulate a flexible model <biological cell> with $20\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}}$ retained particles and an external reservoir with $10\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}}$ retained particles plus $10\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}}$ freely permeating neutral <solute>. Initially the total <osmolarities> are equal. Suppose the permeating <solute> eventually has equal internal and external <concentrations>, there is negligible <pressure> difference, no <active transport>, and the internal retained particle amount is conserved. The permeating contributions cancel from the final osmotic balance; the internal retained <concentration> must fall from $20$ to $10\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}}$. The required <volume> is twice its initial value. This illustrates an initially equal-<osmolarity> but <hypotonic solution>; it assumes the boundary can survive that expansion and predicts no universal time course.

= Hypotonic solution
{parent=Tonicity}

A <hypotonic solution> favors initial net <water> entry under the reference conditions above. A susceptible <biological cell> may undergo <osmotic lysis>; a sufficiently strong <cell wall> can instead support <turgor pressure>.

= Hypertonic solution
{parent=Tonicity}

A <hypertonic solution> favors initial net <water> loss under the reference conditions above. In a walled <plant> cell, enough loss can produce <plasmolysis>.

= Isotonic solution
{parent=Tonicity}

An <isotonic solution> produces no sustained change in cellular <volume> under the stated comparison conditions. <Water> can still exchange in both directions.

= Cytolysis
{parent=Osmosis}
{wiki}

= Osmotic lysis
{synonym}

<Osmotic lysis> is rupture after excessive <water> entry and swelling. It is an outcome for susceptible <biological cells>, not an inevitable response of every <biological cell> to a <hypotonic solution>. <Cell walls> can bear the resulting load, and cellular mechanisms can regulate <water> balance. A <protoplast> challenge tests a particular preparation under particular conditions.

= Active transport
{parent=Membrane transport}
{wiki}

<Active transport> moves a substance uphill in its <electrochemical gradient> by coupling the transfer to a downhill process. Some <carrier proteins> use <ATP> <hydrolysis>; others couple uphill transfer of one <solute> to downhill transfer of another <solute>, often an <ion>. The latter mechanism uses a stored <electrochemical gradient> and need not consume <ATP> directly at that <carrier protein>. Maintaining the driving <electrochemical gradient> still requires a source of <energy>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26896/[Alberts et al.: carrier proteins and active membrane transport].

For the neutral 8:2 example, B-to-A transfer costs $RT\ln4\simeq1.38629RT$ per mole. As an explicitly assumed bookkeeping example, suppose a tightly coupled cycle supplies a downhill <free energy> change of $-2RT$ per mole of transferred <solute>. The total is then $\Delta G_{\mathrm{cycle}}=RT\ln4-2RT\simeq-0.61371RT$. This makes forward cycling energetically possible under those assumptions; it specifies neither a molecular mechanism nor a rate. Merely adding a <carrier protein> cannot supply the missing <free energy>. Conversely, as the transported <concentration> ratio rises, the required uphill cost rises, so a fixed driving process has a finite thermodynamic limit.

= Exocytosis
{parent=Membrane transport}
{wiki}

<Exocytosis> releases the contents of a membrane-bounded compartment to the extracellular space by fusion with the <cell membrane>. <Synaptic vesicle> fusion is an example. Its contents are released, while its membrane becomes connected to the <cell membrane>; see <synaptic vesicle> for recovery and reuse.

= Endocytosis
{parent=Membrane transport}
{wiki}

<Endocytosis> takes material into a <biological cell> by inward budding and separation of part of its <cell membrane>. At a <chemical synapse>, it helps recover membrane and <proteins> after <synaptic vesicle> <exocytosis>. This recovery is distinct from uptake of individual <neurotransmitter> <molecules> through <membrane transport proteins>.

= Fluid mosaic model
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

The <fluid mosaic model> describes a <cell membrane> as a fluid <lipid bilayer> with embedded <membrane proteins>. It connects a continuous barrier to a mosaic of functional components: <diffusion> within the membrane can coexist with selective passage across it. S. J. Singer and G. L. Nicolson presented the <fluid mosaic model> in Science in 1972; see https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4333397/[Singer and Nicolson, The fluid mosaic model of the structure of cell membranes]. The useful picture does not require every <protein> to move freely: attachments and interactions can restrict movement, as discussed in https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9898/[Cooper, The Cell: structure of the plasma membrane].

= Cytoplasm
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

The <cytoplasm> is the material inside a <cell membrane> but outside the <cell nucleus>, when one is present. It includes the <cytosol>, the <organelles> suspended in it, and other structures; it is not just a name for the liquid. In a <prokaryote>, which has no <cell nucleus>, the <nucleoid> and <ribosomes> are within the <cytoplasm>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 4.3: cytoplasm and cytosol] and https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 4.2: prokaryotic organization].

\Image[eukaryotic-cell-compartments.png]
{title=<Cell nucleus>, <cytosol> and <organelles> in a nucleated <eukaryotic> <biological cell>}
{description=Original schematic: the pale blue region represents <cytosol>, while the surrounding <cytoplasm> also includes the depicted <mitochondrion> and <ribosomes>. The <cell nucleus> and <cell nucleolus> are excluded from the <cytoplasm>. A <nuclear pore complex> provides a route across the double <nuclear envelope>. Dimensions and component counts are illustrative; many structures are omitted.}

Try classifying four positions in the diagram. A point in the pale blue region belongs to both <cytosol> and <cytoplasm>. A point inside the <mitochondrion> belongs to an <organelle> included in the <cytoplasm>, but not to the surrounding <cytosol>. A point on a free <ribosome> belongs to the <cytoplasm>, but the <ribosome> itself is not <cytosol>. A point inside the <cell nucleus> belongs to neither <cytoplasm> nor <cytosol>. These distinctions prevent the misleading inference that everything outside the <cell nucleus> shares one undivided liquid environment.

The usefulness of the <cytoplasm> is this combination of shared surroundings and specialized structures: <enzymes> in the <cytosol> carry out many steps of <metabolism>, while <organelles> maintain their own internal conditions. A soluble <protein> made by a free <ribosome> may work in the <cytosol> or be transported elsewhere, including into the <cell nucleus>. Cellular organization therefore combines separation with exchange, rather than assigning each <protein> permanently to its place of synthesis. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26932/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell (2002): traffic between nucleus and cytosol].

= Cytosol
{parent=Cytoplasm}
{wiki}

The <cytosol> is the aqueous part of the <cytoplasm> surrounding its structures, including <organelles>. It contains <water>, <ions> and dissolved <molecules>, including many <proteins>; it is a crowded working environment rather than pure <water>. The liquid inside a <cell nucleus> or a membrane-bounded <organelle> is not the surrounding <cytosol>.

= Nucleoid
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <nucleoid> is the region occupied by <chromosome> <DNA> in a <prokaryotic> <biological cell>. It has no surrounding <nuclear envelope>, but its <DNA> is organized rather than freely spread at random. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells[OpenStax Biology 2e, 4.2: the nucleoid].

= Organelle
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

An <organelle> is a specialized subcellular structure. Usage varies over whether non-membrane-bounded structures such as <ribosomes> count as <organelles>, so explicitly say membrane-bounded <organelles> when comparing <eukaryotic> compartmentalization with <prokaryotic> organization. <Mitochondria> and <chloroplasts> are membrane-bounded examples. See https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells[OpenStax Microbiology, 3.4: cellular compartments].

= Endoplasmic reticulum
{parent=Organelle}
{wiki}

= ER
{c}
{synonym}

= Plastid
{parent=Organelle}
{wiki}

A <plastid> is an <organelle> of the lineage that includes <chloroplasts>. A <chloroplast> is a photosynthetic <plastid>; the terms are not interchangeable because some <plastids> are nonphotosynthetic. <Endosymbiotic theory> connects <plastid> origins to <cyanobacteria>, while <endosymbiotic gene transfer> and <organelle protein import> explain genetic and functional integration with the host.

= Mitochondria
{parent=Organelle}
{wiki}

= Mitochondrion
{synonym}

A <mitochondrion> is an <organelle> whose inner membrane houses machinery for much of aerobic <cellular respiration> and <ATP> production in typical <eukaryotic> cells. <Mitochondria> descend from a bacterial <endosymbiosis> and retain a <genome> and <ribosomes>, although many required <proteins> are encoded by nuclear <genes>. This combination connects compartmentalized function to <evolution>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/23-1-eukaryotic-origins[OpenStax Biology 2e, 23.1: mitochondrial ancestry].

= Chloroplast
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <chloroplast> is a compartment in photosynthetic <plant> and <algae> <biological cells>. Its internal <thylakoids> carry out <light-dependent reactions>; its surrounding <chloroplast stroma> contains the <enzymes> of the <Calvin cycle>. <Cyanobacteria> perform <oxygenic photosynthesis> without <chloroplasts>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26819/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: chloroplasts and photosynthesis].

= Chloroplast stroma
{parent=Chloroplast}

The <chloroplast stroma> is the fluid compartment inside the <chloroplast> envelope but outside its <thylakoids>. The <Calvin cycle> operates here; the interior of a <thylakoid> is a separate compartment.

= Thylakoid
{parent=Chloroplast}
{wiki}

A <thylakoid> is a flattened membrane-bounded compartment. In a <chloroplast>, its membrane contains the machinery for <light-dependent reactions>, including <chlorophyll>. Stacks of <thylakoids> pack membrane area into a small space within the <chloroplast>; the <light-dependent reactions> schematic simplifies this architecture.

= Cell nucleus
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

The <cell nucleus> is a membrane-bounded compartment characteristic of <eukaryotic> <biological cells>, containing most of the cellular <DNA> in a typical nucleated <biological cell>. Its double <nuclear envelope> separates this <DNA> from the surrounding <cytoplasm>. Most does not mean all: <mitochondria> and <chloroplasts> retain their own <DNA>. Nor does every specialized <eukaryotic> <biological cell> retain a <cell nucleus>: mature mammalian <red blood cells> have lost theirs. <Prokaryotes> organize their <DNA> in a <nucleoid> without a <nuclear envelope>, and still have <ribosomes>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Nucleus[NHGRI: nucleus], https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/23-1-eukaryotic-origins[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 23.1: organelle DNA]{ref} and https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/40-2-components-of-the-blood[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 40.2: mature mammalian red blood cells].

Follow one nuclear <protein>-coding <gene>: its <DNA> is used in <transcription> to make <RNA>; after the appropriate processing, <messenger RNA> is exported through a <nuclear pore complex>; a <ribosome> in the <cytoplasm> then uses it in <biological translation> to build an <amino acid> chain. The <DNA> remains in the <cell nucleus>. The <RNA> is a template, not the material converted into a <protein>. The <ribosome> diagram below follows this route; <gene expression> gives a complementary <nucleotide sequence> example. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26932/[Alberts et al.: selective RNA export and protein import].

This architecture makes <gene expression> interesting in a spatial way: nuclear <transcription> and cytoplasmic <biological translation> are separated by a selective boundary. A <messenger RNA> can be processed before export and use, while <proteins> needed for nuclear functions travel inward. Inside the <cell nucleus>, the <cell nucleolus> helps assemble <ribosome> subunits for export. Thus the compartment housing most <DNA> also helps build the machinery that interprets its messages elsewhere.

Robert Brown described and named the <cell nucleus> in his account of <plant> <biological cells>, read to the Linnean Society in 1831 and published in its Transactions in 1833. This historical observation identified a recurring structure, without establishing its later-understood role in <DNA> storage or <gene expression>. See https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1095-8339.1829.tb00158.x[Brown, On the Organs and Mode of Fecundation in Orchideæ and Asclepiadeæ, Transactions 16, 685–738 (1833)]. This is a documented contribution rather than a claim that no one had observed the structure earlier.

= Nuclear envelope
{parent=Cell nucleus}
{wiki}

The <nuclear envelope> is the double-membrane boundary around a <cell nucleus>. Regulated passage connects nuclear processes to the surrounding <cytoplasm>: <transcription> produces <RNA> in the <cell nucleus>, while exported <messenger RNA> can undergo <biological translation> on <ribosomes> outside it. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Nuclear-Membrane[NHGRI: nuclear membrane].

= Nuclear pore complex
{parent=Nuclear envelope}
{wiki}

= Nuclear pore
{synonym}

A <nuclear pore complex> is an assembly of <proteins> spanning the <nuclear envelope> and regulating passage between the <cell nucleus> and <cytosol>. It is a selective gateway, not simply an unregulated hole: <messenger RNA> export and import of nuclear <proteins> require appropriate transport machinery. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26932/[Alberts et al.: nuclear pore complexes].

= Nucleolus
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Cell nucleus}

= Cell nucleolus
{synonym}

The <cell nucleolus> is a region inside a <cell nucleus> without a surrounding membrane, where much <ribosomal RNA> is made and processed and <ribosome> subunits begin to assemble with <proteins>. The subunits are exported to the <cytoplasm>; the <cell nucleolus> is not itself a translating <ribosome>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 4.3: nucleolus and ribosomal subunits].

= Ribosome
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <ribosome> is the molecular machinery for <biological translation>: it reads <messenger RNA> and joins <amino acids> into a <polypeptide> that can become a <protein>. Both <prokaryotes> and <eukaryotes> have <ribosomes>. A <ribosome> consists of <ribosomal RNA> and <proteins> organized into small and large subunits, with no enclosing membrane. <Ribosomal RNA> contributes to both structure and the <active site> that forms <peptide bonds>; a <ribosome> is therefore not merely a collection of <proteins>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 4.3: ribosome composition] and https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10937990/[Nissen et al., The structural basis of ribosome activity in peptide bond synthesis, Science 289, 920–930 (2000)].

\Image[ribosome-translation-schematic.png]
{title=Nuclear <transcription>, <RNA> export and <ribosome>-mediated <biological translation>}
{description=Original schematic of a nuclear <protein>-coding <gene> in a nucleated <eukaryotic> <biological cell>. The upper route tracks <DNA> to <RNA> by <transcription>, <RNA> export, then <biological translation>. The lower example reads three <messenger RNA> <codons> from $5'$ to $3'$ and shows a growing <polypeptide> segment from its <N-terminus> toward its <C-terminus>. Shapes and distances are illustrative; processing, flanking signals, additional machinery and termination are omitted.}

For a computed decoding example, choose the <reading frame> starting at the first displayed <nucleotide> of $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUG\ GCU\ GAA}\text{-}3'$. Under the standard <genetic code>, AUG specifies <methionine> (Met), GCU specifies <alanine> (Ala), and GAA specifies a <glutamic acid residue> (Glu) in the growing <polypeptide>. Nine <nucleotides> divide into three <codons>, giving the ordered three-<amino acid residue> segment Met–Ala–Glu. The <ribosome> reads the <messenger RNA> $5'\to3'$, and the chain grows from its <N-terminus> toward its <C-terminus>. If synthesis begins at this AUG in an appropriate <eukaryotic> initiation context, these are the first three <amino acid residues> before any subsequent processing. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 15.1: standard codon assignments].

For this three-<amino acid residue> segment, joining the three delivered <amino acids> requires two <peptide bonds>, one between Met and Ala and another between Ala and Glu. There is no <stop codon> in the displayed <nucleotide sequence>, so this is a growing segment rather than a complete released <protein>. An isolated nine-<nucleotide> <RNA> is not claimed to supply all signals needed for initiation. This small calculation separates three jobs: the <messenger RNA> specifies order, <transfer RNA> delivers the <amino acids>, and the <ribosome> catalyzes their joining. Each new <amino acid residue> is added at the <C-terminus>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-5-ribosomes-and-protein-synthesis[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 15.5: initiation and chain elongation].

The beauty of a <ribosome> is its reusable chemistry: the same machinery can assemble many different <proteins> by following different <messenger RNA> templates. The <genetic code> connects an alphabet of <nucleotides> to an alphabet of <amino acids>, while <transfer RNA> links recognition of the message to delivery of building material. Changing the message can therefore change the product without building a different <ribosome> for every <protein>.

In 1955 George E. Palade described small particles in the <cytoplasm>, both free and associated with membranes, in https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2223592/[A small particulate component of the cytoplasm, Journal of Biophysical and Biochemical Cytology 1, 59–68]. The paper documents the structural observations, rather than the full modern decoding mechanism. Complementary biochemical work by Hoagland, Stephenson, Scott, Hecht and Zamecnik described a soluble <RNA> intermediate in <protein> synthesis in 1958, helping establish the role now assigned to <transfer RNA>; see https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/13538965/[A soluble ribonucleic acid intermediate in protein synthesis, Journal of Biological Chemistry 231, 241–257]. Together, structural observations and biochemical experiments connected visible cellular machinery with its working parts.

= Gliding bacterium
{parent=Biological cell}

A <gliding bacterium> moves along a surface without swimming through the bulk fluid. In a thin-fluid-film model, its relative motion dissipates energy through <Couette flow>, although the actuation mechanism can involve additional dissipation.

= Neutrophil
{parent=Biological cell}

A <neutrophil> is an immune <biological cell>. Its adhesive rolling on a surface involves contact mechanics, fluid stresses, and binding and unbinding of adhesive bonds.

= Red blood cell
{parent=Biological cell}

A red blood cell is a specialized <biological cell> that transports <oxygen> in blood. Its deformability lets it pass through vessels narrower than its unstressed size. A compliant-body <lubrication> model isolates the coupling between its deformation, the surrounding viscous film and a driving <pressure> difference; it is an idealization rather than a complete membrane or cytoplasm model.

= Membrane potential
{parent=Biological cell}
{title2=$V=V_{\mathrm{inside}}-V_{\mathrm{outside}}$}
{wiki}

The voltage difference across a cell membrane. Ion concentration gradients and selective membrane conductances determine it; changing conductance can change the voltage.

= Depolarization
{parent=Membrane potential}
{wiki}

For a negative neuronal <membrane potential>, <depolarization> raises it toward zero or beyond, making the interior less negative relative to the exterior. This describes a voltage change, not necessarily increased <action potential> generation; see the depolarizing inhibition example under <synaptic potential>.

= Hyperpolarization
{parent=Membrane potential}

<Hyperpolarization> makes a neuronal <membrane potential> more negative relative to the exterior. It is a voltage description; inhibition need not produce <hyperpolarization>, as <shunting inhibition> illustrates.

= Reversal potential
{parent=Membrane potential}
{wiki}
{title2=$I=g(E_{\mathrm{rev}}-V)$}

A <reversal potential> $E_{\mathrm{rev}}$ is a <membrane potential> at which a specified open <ion channel>, or specified combination of <ion channels>, carries zero net <electric current>, with the surrounding conditions fixed; the <electric current> changes sign across that voltage. Specifying which current matters: an individual <ion> contribution and a sum of several contributions can reverse at different voltages. Zero net <electric current> does not mean that all <ions> have stopped moving. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11013/[Purves et al., Neuroscience, 2nd edition (2001): reversal of a mixed-ion channel current].

Throughout these examples, <membrane potential> is $V=V_{\mathrm{inside}}-V_{\mathrm{outside}}$, and \b[<conventional current> is inward-positive]. Positive <electric current> corresponds to net positive <electric charge> entering or net negative <electric charge> leaving. Negative <electric current> corresponds to the reverse charge transfer. A convenient Ohmic approximation is
$$
I_{\mathrm{in}}=g(E_{\mathrm{rev}}-V),\qquad g\geq0,
$$
where $g$ is the available <electrical conductance>. For fixed $g>0$, $V<E_{\mathrm{rev}}$ gives positive <electric current>, $V=E_{\mathrm{rev}}$ gives zero, and $V>E_{\mathrm{rev}}$ gives negative <electric current>; the line has slope $-g$. If $g=0$, the modeled pathway carries zero <electric current> at every voltage: closing it does not locate its <reversal potential>. <Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/3.1 Synapses> (§3.1: conductance-based synaptic current) uses outward-positive $g(V-E)$; the formula here reverses that sign to match this wiki's inward-positive convention.

For the stipulated single-pathway example from <ion channel>, $E_{\mathrm{rev}}=-80\,\mathrm{mV}$ and $g=10\,\mathrm{nS}$ give
$$
\begin{array}{c|rrr}
V\ (\mathrm{mV})&-100&-80&-60\\
E_{\mathrm{rev}}-V\ (\mathrm{mV})&20&0&-20\\
I_{\mathrm{in}}\ (\mathrm{pA})&200&0&-200
\end{array}
$$
The open <ion channel> carries zero net <electric current> at $-80\,\mathrm{mV}$ even though $g$ remains positive. Near that point a change of <membrane potential> changes the <electric current>, which is why zero current at one voltage does not make an open pathway electrically irrelevant; see <shunting inhibition>.

\Image[ion-channel-current-and-reversal.png]
{title=Inward-positive <electric current>: one reversal and two-channel cancellation}
{description=Original computed curves for stipulated constant <electrical conductances> and fixed <reversal potentials>. Left: $g=10\,\mathrm{nS}$, $E_{\mathrm{rev}}=-80\,\mathrm{mV}$. Right: a <potassium> pathway with $g_{\mathrm K}=10\,\mathrm{nS}$, $E_{\mathrm K}=-80\,\mathrm{mV}$ and a <sodium> pathway with $g_{\mathrm{Na}}=2\,\mathrm{nS}$, $E_{\mathrm{Na}}=+50\,\mathrm{mV}$. Their net <electric current> vanishes at $-58.3333\,\mathrm{mV}$ while both individual <electric currents> remain nonzero. These are model values, not empirical measurements.}

For an <ion channel> passing only one <ion> species by <passive transport>, its <reversal potential> can equal that species' <Nernst equilibrium potential>, where the chemical and electrical contributions to <electrochemical potential> balance. At that <thermodynamic equilibrium>, passage in both directions can continue with no net transport. For an <ion channel> admitting several <ion> species, opposing contributions can cancel while each species continues to have net transport. Such a <reversal potential> is not generally any one species' <Nernst equilibrium potential>. See the Purves reference above.

To work out that cancellation explicitly, put two Ohmic <ion channel> populations across the same <cell membrane>. Stipulate constant <electrical conductances> and fixed <reversal potentials>:
$$
g_{\mathrm K}=10\,\mathrm{nS},\quad E_{\mathrm K}=-80\,\mathrm{mV},\qquad g_{\mathrm{Na}}=2\,\mathrm{nS},\quad E_{\mathrm{Na}}=+50\,\mathrm{mV}.
$$
The <potassium> and <sodium> contributions add because both pathways experience the same <membrane potential>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
I_{\mathrm{total}}&=g_{\mathrm K}(E_{\mathrm K}-V)+g_{\mathrm{Na}}(E_{\mathrm{Na}}-V)\\
&=(g_{\mathrm K}+g_{\mathrm{Na}})(E_{\mathrm{eff}}-V),\\
E_{\mathrm{eff}}&=\frac{g_{\mathrm K}E_{\mathrm K}+g_{\mathrm{Na}}E_{\mathrm{Na}}}{g_{\mathrm K}+g_{\mathrm{Na}}}
=\frac{-700}{12}\,\mathrm{mV}\approx-58.3333\,\mathrm{mV}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus $I_{\mathrm{total}}=12\,\mathrm{nS}(E_{\mathrm{eff}}-V)$. This <weighted mean> follows simply by expanding and regrouping the two current terms; it assumes additive Ohmic contributions and a positive total <electrical conductance>. At $V=E_{\mathrm{eff}}$, the inward-positive individual <electric currents> are
$$
I_{\mathrm K}=-\frac{650}{3}\,\mathrm{pA}\approx-216.6667\,\mathrm{pA},\qquad
I_{\mathrm{Na}}=+\frac{650}{3}\,\mathrm{pA}\approx+216.6667\,\mathrm{pA}.
$$
The <potassium> contribution is outward and the <sodium> contribution inward. Equal and opposite <electric currents> give $I_{\mathrm{total}}=0$; neither pathway has stopped transporting its <ions>. The calculation treats two distinct pathways. Applying it to one mixed-species <ion channel> would additionally require a justified decomposition into independent Ohmic contributions; a general mixed-channel <reversal potential> cannot be obtained by assuming those hypotheses silently.

This is an instantaneous balance of the specified passive <electric currents>. It would be the stationary voltage of a passive <capacitance> model containing only these constant pathways and no applied <electric current>, but it is not a complete prediction of a living cell's resting <membrane potential>. Other <electric currents>, voltage-dependent gating, and pumps involved in <active transport> can matter. Pumps that transfer net <electric charge> are electrogenic and contribute their own <electric current>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK10857/[Purves et al.: functional properties of the sodium/potassium pump].

The toy model holds <ion> <concentrations> and therefore the stipulated <reversal potentials> fixed. Continuing inward <sodium> and outward <potassium> transport would change finite reservoirs unless <active transport> or external control maintained them. Transport can be a small fraction of the reservoir's <ions> over a short interval, making fixed <concentrations> a useful approximation; it is not a claim that the reservoirs never change. See https://neuronaldynamics.epfl.ch/online/Ch2.S1.html[Gerstner et al., Neuronal Dynamics (2014), §2.1: equilibrium potentials, channel currents and maintained gradients].

A <reversal potential> makes the effect of opening an <ion channel> predictable from the present <membrane potential>. The same open pathway can carry inward or outward <electric current> as $V$ crosses its <reversal potential>. The two-pathway example reveals a particularly useful distinction: zero net charge transfer can coexist with continuing exchange of different substances. That distinction connects <membrane transport> to <electric current> measurements without mistaking electrical balance for <thermodynamic equilibrium>.

= Nernst equilibrium potential
{c}
{parent=Reversal potential}
{title2=$E_{\mathrm{ion}}=\frac{RT}{zF}\ln\!\left(\frac{a_{\mathrm{outside}}}{a_{\mathrm{inside}}}\right)$}

The <Nernst equilibrium potential> is the inside-minus-outside <membrane potential> at which one <ion> species has equal <electrochemical potential> on both sides of a <cell membrane>. At uniform <temperature> $T$, using the same reference <chemical potential> on both sides, $z\neq0$ is the signed <ion> charge number, $R$ the <molar gas constant>, $F$ the <Faraday constant>, and $a$ the dimensionless <thermodynamic activity> of that <ion>. For an ideal dilute <chemical solution>, the activity ratio becomes the <concentration> ratio. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11102/[Purves et al.: the forces that create membrane potentials and the Nernst equation].

The short derivation is to set the outside-to-inside <electrochemical potential> change from <membrane transport> to zero: $RT\ln(a_{\mathrm{inside}}/a_{\mathrm{outside}})+zFV=0$, and solve for $V$. An open pathway carrying only this species by <passive transport> then has this <reversal potential>. A balance of several nonzero <ion> <electric currents> does not establish this equilibrium condition for each species.

= Voltage-gated ion channel
{parent=Membrane potential}
{wiki}

A membrane channel whose opening depends on <membrane potential>. Regenerative activation and subsequent inactivation or opposing currents help produce an <action potential>.

= Ion-channel inactivation
{parent=Voltage-gated ion channel}

<Ion-channel inactivation> is loss of availability for conduction, distinct from simply closing an otherwise activatable <ion channel>. Recovery depends on channel type and <membrane potential>; incomplete recovery of <sodium>-selective <voltage-gated ion channels> contributes to a <neuronal refractory period>.

= Two-state ion-channel gating
{parent=Voltage-gated ion channel}
{title2=$\dot X=\alpha_X(V)(1-X)-\beta_X(V)X$}

<Two-state ion-channel gating> models an individual gate as a random switch between closed and open states. Write $p(t)$ for its open <probability>; this is the $X$ in the header equation. An individual gate's state is always closed or open, rather than partly open. A population of identical gates can have a fraction open near $p(t)$, and averaging repeated identically prepared trials estimates the same <probability>. A finite population's observed fraction fluctuates. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK10841/[Purves and colleagues, Neuroscience, 2nd edition (2001): Ion Channels Underlying Action Potentials] for single-channel steps and their population averages.

At fixed <membrane potential> $V$, let constant rates $\alpha,\beta>0$ govern closed-to-open and open-to-closed transitions respectively, in $\mathrm{s}^{-1}$. This is a two-state <continuous-time Markov chain>. Over a short interval $\Delta t$, an initially closed gate opens with <probability> $\alpha\Delta t+o(\Delta t)$, and an initially open gate closes with <probability> $\beta\Delta t+o(\Delta t)$. Gain minus loss gives the deterministic <ordinary differential equation> for the <probability>:
$$
\frac{dp}{dt}=\alpha(1-p)-\beta p=\frac{p_\infty-p}{\tau},\qquad
p_\infty=\frac{\alpha}{\alpha+\beta},\qquad \tau=\frac{1}{\alpha+\beta}.
$$
Here $p_\infty$ is the fixed-voltage limiting open <probability>, and $\tau$ is a <time constant>. The ratio of the rates fixes the limit, while their sum fixes the speed. With initial <probability> $p(0)=p_0\in[0,1]$,
$$
p(t)=p_\infty+(p_0-p_\infty)e^{-t/\tau}.
$$
For a short derivation, subtract $p_\infty$: the deviation obeys $d(p-p_\infty)/dt=-(p-p_\infty)/\tau$, yielding the <exponential function>. After one <time constant>, $1/e$ of the initial deviation remains. The solution stays between $p_0$ and $p_\infty$, so it remains a valid <probability>. The rate and <time constant> formulations are described in https://neuronaldynamics.epfl.ch/online/Ch2.S2.html[Gerstner and colleagues, Neuronal Dynamics, §2.2.2: transition rates and channel kinetics].

For a worked toy condition A, choose $\alpha=\beta=50\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $p_0=0.1$. Then $p_\infty=0.5$, $\tau=0.01\,\mathrm{s}=10\,\mathrm{ms}$, and
$$
p(10\,\mathrm{ms})=0.5-0.4e^{-1}\approx0.352848.
$$
In a separately prepared condition B, choose $\alpha=200\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, $\beta=50\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and the same $p_0=0.1$. Now $p_\infty=0.8$, $\tau=4\,\mathrm{ms}$, and $p(10\,\mathrm{ms})=0.8-0.7e^{-2.5}\approx0.742540$. Condition B approaches a higher limit more quickly. These are two stipulated constant-rate conditions with $V$ held fixed in each experiment, not an empirical function relating rates to voltage or a sequential switch between conditions. The initial $p_0=0.1$ need not be the limiting <probability> at the newly imposed voltage.

\Image[ion-channel-gating-relaxation.png]
{title=<Two-state ion-channel gating>: two constant-rate relaxation conditions}
{description=Original computed <probability> curves, both starting at $p_0=0.1$. Dashed lines show the limiting open <probabilities> $0.5$ and $0.8$; dotted lines mark <time constants> $10$ and $4\,\mathrm{ms}$. The dot marks condition A at $10\,\mathrm{ms}$, where $p\approx0.352848$. Smooth curves represent open <probability> or population averages, not an individual gate's random state trace or an ionic <electric current>.}

In the <Hodgkin-Huxley model>, the rates depend on <membrane potential>: $\alpha_X(V)$ and $\beta_X(V)$. Its $X_\infty(V)=\alpha_X(V)/(\alpha_X(V)+\beta_X(V))$ is the limit for voltage held at that $V$. During an <action potential>, changing $V$ changes the rates and gates feed back onto $V$ through ionic <electric currents>; substituting the present voltage into the constant-rate solution does not solve that coupled system. Gating describes availability of a conducting pathway. Even an open <ion channel> carries zero net ionic <electric current> at its <reversal potential>.

The useful bridge is from abrupt random molecular events to a smooth population prediction. A channel requiring several <independent permissive gates in an ion channel> has an open <probability> equal to their product; this can make channel availability much smaller than each gate's availability. The <Hodgkin-Huxley model> uses $\bar g_{\mathrm{Na}}m^3h$ and $\bar g_{\mathrm K}n^4$ for <sodium> and <potassium> <electrical conductances>, respectively.

= Independent permissive gates in an ion channel
{parent=Two-state ion-channel gating}
{title2=$P_{\mathrm{open}}=p^q$}

A permissive gate allows conduction when it is open. Suppose an <ion channel> conducts only when all $q$ gates are open, where $q$ is a positive <integer>. If the gate-open <events> are mutually <independent events> and each has the same open <probability> $p$ at the time considered, then
$$
P_{\mathrm{open}}=\prod_{j=1}^q p=p^q.
$$
This follows from the multiplication rule for <independent events>. Identical transition rates alone do not guarantee independence: the initial gate states must also be independently prepared, and the model must not couple their transitions. Gates sharing a fluctuating <membrane potential> can be correlated; the simple product requires an appropriate independence assumption, for example independent gates at an externally fixed voltage.

At $p=0.5$, channels requiring $q=1,2,4$ permissive gates have open <probabilities> $0.5$, $0.25$ and $0.0625$. For four independent gates there are $2^4=16$ possible open/closed configurations, equally probable at $p=0.5$; only the all-open configuration conducts. Thus $0.5$ is the gate-open <probability>, while $0.0625$ is the whole-channel open <probability>. Neither is a fractional state of one observed channel. Among $160$ independently prepared identical four-gate channels, the <expected value> of the open count is $160/16=10$, although an actual count fluctuates.

\Image[independent-ion-channel-gates.png]
{title=<Independent permissive gates in an ion channel>: an all-open requirement}
{description=Left: whole-channel open <probability> $p^q$ for one, two and four identical independent permissive gates. Right: all sixteen configurations of four gates at $p=0.5$, each with <probability> $1/16$; filled circles are open gates and empty circles closed gates. Only the highlighted all-open configuration permits conduction. This is an illustrative model, not a molecular structure or an ionic <electric current> measurement.}

For unequal independent gate-open <probabilities> $p_j$, use $P_{\mathrm{open}}=\prod_j p_j$ rather than $p^q$. This is the distinction behind the <Hodgkin-Huxley model>'s $m^3h$: $m$ and $h$ are different activation and inactivation-availability variables, rather than one common $p$. Its $n^4$ uses four identical activation factors. Independence and the all-open requirement are model assumptions; correlated gates or alternative conducting configurations require a different calculation. The simple product is useful because a modest gate-open <probability> can produce sharply suppressed whole-channel availability without any additional gate interaction.

